  "body_mappings": [
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        498.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        597.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "On the morning of 7 November 1940, a sustained wind of about 40 miles an hour set the Tacoma Narrows Bridge twisting. Its deck tilted from side to side in ever more violent oscillations until most of the main span tore apart and tumbled into the water. No one died. Footage of the spectacle would later fascinate students worldwide."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        410.96141732283473,
        461.8976377952756,
        490.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "The bridge had opened in July 1940 as the third-longest suspension span in the world at the time, its roadway hung from steel cables. Girders only eight feet deep stiffened the deck, making it slender, elegant, and economical, but far from rigid. Even faint breezes made it heave, earning the nickname Galloping Gertie."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        323.26141732283475,
        461.8976377952756,
        402.46141732283473
      ],
      "text": "Textbooks have long invoked resonance: a periodic push that matches an object's natural frequency can amplify its motion, as when well-timed shoves propel a child on a swing. The analogy is intuitive but deceptive. That morning's wind did not pulse in time with the bridge; it blew fairly steadily."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        215.76141732283475,
        461.8976377952756,
        314.76141732283475
      ],
      "text": "The mechanism was subtler. Each twist altered the passing airflow, which reinforced the next twist: a feedback loop that kept tapping the wind for energy. In March 1941 a federal panel blamed the deck's flexibility and wing-like lift. Later analysis called the mechanism flutter; in February 1991 two engineers asserted in a physics journal that textbooks had erred."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        108.26141732283475,
        461.8976377952756,
        207.26141732283475
      ],
      "text": "The panel urged wind-tunnel tests, and trials on scaled-down replicas gradually became customary for suspension bridges. A sturdier replacement opened in October 1950. Gertie's fall may still instruct engineers, if read accurately: the wind never kept time with the bridge, the bridge's own motion turned a sustained wind destructive."
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        537.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        597.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "1940年11月7日上午，一股时速约40英里的持续大风使塔科马海峡大桥扭动起来。它的桥面左右倾侧，振荡愈发剧烈，直到主跨大部分断裂、并且跌落到水中。无人丧生。这一奇观的影像后来吸引了世界各地的学生。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        469.1614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        529.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "这座桥于1940年7月通车，作为当时世界上第三长的悬索桥跨径，其路面悬挂在钢缆上。仅八英尺深的钢梁加固了桥面，使其变得纤细、优雅且经济，但远非刚硬。哪怕轻微的微风也会让它起伏，从而赢得了一个绰号：“奔腾的格蒂”。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        400.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        460.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "教科书长期以来一直援引共振来解释：与物体固有频率相匹配的周期性的推力能放大其运动，就像时机恰到好处的一次次猛推推动秋千上的孩子一样。这个类比直观却具有迷惑性。那天上午的风没有与大桥有节奏地跳动；它吹得相当平稳。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        312.1614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        392.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "其机理要更为微妙。每一次扭动都改变了掠过的气流，而气流又加强了下一次扭动：这是一个不断从风中汲取能量的反馈回路。1941年3月，一个联邦调查委员会把原因归于桥面的柔性和类似机翼的升力。后来的分析把这一机理称为颤振；1991年2月，两位工程师在一份物理学期刊上断言，教科书出错了。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        223.66141732283472,
        461.8976377952756,
        303.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "该委员会力主进行风洞试验，并且对按比例缩小的复制品进行试验，对于悬索桥而言也渐渐成为了惯例。一个更坚固的替代品于1950年10月通车（开放）。格蒂的倒塌或许仍能指导着工程师，如果解读得精准：风从未与大桥合拍，是大桥自身的运动使一股持续的风具有了破坏力。"
    }
  ],
